## Founding & History
Frogmore Creek Vineyard was established in 1996. The winery bottled its first vintage in 2002. It was founded on the principle of striving for excellence in farming methods, wine production, service, and hospitality. Frogmore Creek was Tasmania's first certified organic vineyard. The property was developed by a group of Western Australian farmers who conducted extensive research before choosing their Tasmanian location. The winery was previously known as Meadowbank Estate and underwent a rebranding in 2012.

## People
Tony Scherer, an expert in organic farming for over 20 years, manages Frogmore Creek's vineyard. He was a pioneer in the American organic fruit and vegetable industry in the 1970s and founded the Compost Company, the first organic composting company for agriculture in Western Australia. Andrew Hood is the chief winemaker, recognized for improving the quality of Tasmanian wines over the past 15 years. He was a 'Winemaker of the Year' finalist in 2000 and 2004. Alain Rousseau, a French-born winemaker trained in the Loire Valley, also works with the Hood Wines' team, bringing extensive experience to Frogmore Creek. Senior Winemaker Alain Rousseau and Winemaker John Bown are responsible for crafting the wines. Danny Belbin is the Vineyard Manager.

## Vineyard & Site
Frogmore Creek Vineyard is located in Penna, within the Coal River Valley region of southern Tasmania, about a 30-minute drive from Hobart. The property spans 436 hectares (1077 acres), with 180 hectares (445 acres) suitable for vines. The vineyard's soil and climate statistics are comparable to those of Champagne, Bordeaux, and Burgundy. Critical climate indicators such as rainfall, temperature, sunshine hours, and humidity combine to make it a premium quality wine area. The vineyard was Tasmania's first certified organic vineyard. Current varietals planted include Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling, Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc, and Pinot Gris. The viticulture team is committed to environmentally sustainable practices. Grapes are sustainably grown and harvested from selected blocks.

## Cellar Door Experience
The Cellar Door is open seven days a week from 10 am to 5 pm, except on Christmas Day. For 1-7 guests, wine tastings are walk-in and do not require bookings. For groups of 8-25 guests, bookings are required and the experience is privately hosted in the Flawed Gallery or on the Terrace for $15 per person. This group tasting includes a welcome, history and wine information, and tasting of four wines with a Cellar Master, lasting up to one hour. Individual wine tastings for 1-7 guests cost $10 per person, with $5 refundable upon wine purchase. The Cellar Door is housed in a rustic homestead with views over rolling hills and the vineyard. It offers an extensive list of premium wines on tasting.

## Food & Hospitality
Frogmore Creek features a restaurant that offers a modern Australian menu, showcasing fresh, locally sourced produce. The restaurant is open Wednesday to Monday from 11:30 am to 3:00 pm. On Tuesdays, a limited menu of oysters and cheese boards is available from 10 am to 4 pm. Reservations are recommended for the restaurant. The venue can cater to all dietary requirements with 48 hours prior notice, with a portion of the seasonal menu being gluten-free and/or vegetarian. The restaurant offers captivating views of the vineyard. Catering packages for events draw influence from Tasmanian land, sea, and gardens. The dining style is described as casual elegant, with a neat and tidy dress code and footwear required. Executive Chef is Mark Burne.

## Awards & Recognition
Frogmore Creek has received numerous awards and recognition for its wines and restaurant. Wines made by Chief Winemaker Andrew Hood consistently win more than half of all awards at Tasmanian wine shows. The 2002 Reserve Pinot Noir won Best Pinot and Best Wine of Show at the 2004 Tasmanian Wine Show and was awarded 96 points by James Halliday in his 2005 Australian Wine Companion. The Frogmore Creek 2005 Pinot Noir received the Chairman's 'Best of Class' at the 2007 Long Beach Grand Cru Wine Competition and a Gold Medal at the 2007 Los Angeles International Wine and Spirits Competition. In October 2024, Frogmore Creek Wines won the 'Best Wine of the 2024 Challenge' at the 23rd annual Canberra International Riesling Challenge for its 2023 Frogmore Creek Iced Riesling, also earning Best Sweet Riesling and Best Australian Riesling. Their Pinot Noir also received a Gold medal at the Australian Pinot Noir Challenge in October 2024. In October 2025, the 2024 42 Degrees South Pinot Noir claimed the Tasmania Trophy at the 2025 Australian Pinot Noir Challenge. In 2015, Frogmore Creek was awarded Restaurant of the Year Tasmanian and Restaurant of the Year for Regional Australia.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Frogmore Creek Vineyard was Tasmania's first certified organic vineyard. The viticulture team is deeply committed to environmentally sustainable practices, viewing themselves as custodians of the land. They focus on maintaining the health of the soils and vines. All wines from the 2014 vintage onwards are certified Vegan. The winery emphasizes a philosophy of environmental sustainability.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address is 699 Richmond Road, Cambridge, Tasmania, 7170. It is located approximately 15-20 minutes drive from Hobart's CBD. It is also about 10 kilometers or 10 minutes drive from the Hobart Airport. An Uber from Hobart Airport is approximately $25, and from Hobart CBD, it's about $35. A public bus service is also available from Hobart CBD. The phone number is (03) 6274 5844. The website is www.frogmorecreek.com.au. The venue is wheelchair accessible, with designated parking and a ramped entry. However, accessing the main entrance might be challenging due to stairs, and a side entrance may require assistance to open both doors for wheelchair access.

## Notable / Distinctive
Frogmore Creek was Tasmania's first certified organic vineyard. It is described as the largest, ultra-premium, cool-climate winery in Southern Tasmania. The vineyard's location in the Coal River Valley has soil and climate statistics almost identical to internationally distinguished benchmarks like Champagne, Bordeaux, and Burgundy. The winery operates under a winemaking philosophy of "letting the fruit do the talking". The Cellar Door features an art gallery on the mezzanine floor, known as the 'Flawed History' art gallery, which provides an overview of wine history in Tasmania. Frogmore Creek offers versatile event facilities, including the Barrel Room with an open fireplace and floor-to-ceiling windows, suitable for weddings and corporate functions for up to 160 seated guests.

